  "passage": {
    "title": "Social Media and Friendship",
    "paragraphs": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "A decade ago, many people believed that social media would bring the world closer. Today, opinions are more divided. Some researchers say that sites like Instagram and TikTok make it easier to stay in touch, while others claim that they leave us lonelier than ever. This essay looks at both sides."
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "There is no doubt that social media has benefits. Families who live in different countries can share photos and videos instantly. People with unusual hobbies can find others who share them, even if nobody in their town does. Shy people often find it easier to start conversations online than face to face. For these groups, social media can create real friendships."
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "However, there are risks. When people compare their lives with the perfect pictures of others, they can feel unhappy about their own. One American study found that students who spent more than three hours a day on social media were more likely to report feeling lonely. Also, a message is not the same as a conversation. Without a voice or a face, it is easy to misunderstand what a friend really means."
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Does this mean we should delete our accounts? Probably not. The evidence suggests that the way we use these sites matters more than the time we spend on them. Writing to a friend or joining a group discussion seems to be good for us, whereas silently looking at other people's photos seems to make us feel worse."
      },
      {
        "label": "E",
        "text": "My own view is that social media works best as an addition to real-life friendships, not a replacement. A good rule is to use it to arrange meetings, and then to put the phone away when you are together."
      }
    ]
  },
